(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypertension-quiz"]={"title":"Hypertension Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What clinical finding should raise suspicion of coarctation of the aorta?","choices":["Unexplained hypokalemia with resistant hypertension","A marked creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor","Episodic sweating with palpitations and pallor","Upper-extremity hypertension with weak or delayed femoral pulses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the confirmation is to compare arm with leg pressures: normally the leg systolic is the higher of the two, so a reversal of that relationship is the telling finding. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"For which blood pressure categories is lifestyle treatment recommended?","choices":["Only once medication has failed","Only for patients with an identifiable secondary cause","Every category","Only stage 2 hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why an elevated reading that does not yet warrant medication still warrants an intervention, and why lifestyle continues alongside drugs rather than being replaced by them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which laboratory clue should prompt a search for a secondary cause?","choices":["A normal thyroid-stimulating hormone","Unexplained hypokalemia","A mildly raised total cholesterol","An isolated raised fasting glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because it points toward primary aldosteronism. Disproportionate organ damage relative to the blood pressure burden and abnormal renal function are the other two laboratory clues listed. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A patient develops a dry cough on an ACE inhibitor. What is the appropriate response?","choices":["Combine the ACE inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker","Stop all antihypertensive therapy","Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker","Add an antitussive and continue the ACE inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cough is bradykinin-mediated and occurs in up to about one in five, and angiotensin receptor blockers do not raise bradykinin, so the effect is lost while the therapeutic action is kept. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"An aldosterone to renin ratio comes back elevated. What is the next step?","choices":["Refer to endocrinology for confirmatory testing","Start spironolactone and take no further action","Proceed directly to adrenalectomy","Repeat the ratio annually with no other action"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ratio is a screen rather than a diagnosis, and confirmation plus subtype work belongs to the specialist, which is what determines whether the patient is a surgical candidate. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What dietary and activity targets are described?","choices":["Sodium restriction alone, with no activity component","High-protein dieting with unrestricted sodium","Moderate alcohol intake for cardiovascular benefit","A DASH-style pattern with reduced sodium and exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside weight reduction, where a sustained loss in the region of five to ten kilograms can meaningfully lower systolic pressure. About 150 minutes a week of moderate aerobic activity plus resistance training is the target. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which patient group is angioedema more common in, and what is the usual substitution?","choices":["Patients with asthma, with a beta blocker substituted","Black patients, with an angiotensin receptor blocker substituted","Older women, with a thiazide substituted","Patients with chronic kidney disease, with a calcium channel blocker substituted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a class effect of ACE inhibition mediated by bradykinin, and angiotensin receptor blockers can usually be used instead, though angioedema with them remains possible. Angioedema is two to four times more common in this group. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which clinical clues point toward Cushing syndrome in a hypertensive patient?","choices":["Episodic headache, sweating and palpitations","Loud snoring and witnessed apneas","Weak femoral pulses with an arm-to-leg pressure difference","Proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising and purple striae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the width of the striae matters: broad purple striae over a centimeter are what distinguish this from the ordinary stretch marks that accompany weight gain alone. Thin skin and central adiposity complete the picture. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"By how much should mean arterial pressure be reduced in the first hour of a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["By no more than about twenty to twenty-five percent","To below 130/80 within the first hour","By at least half, to remove the immediate danger","No reduction until imaging is complete"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with a target around 160/100 to 110 over two to six hours and gradual normalization over a day or two. Aortic dissection and some stroke syndromes are excepted from this general approach. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which cerebrovascular events does hypertension predispose to?","choices":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage exclusively","Both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke","Ischemic stroke only","Intracerebral hemorrhage only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It raises the risk of both mechanisms rather than one, and chronic small-vessel injury separately contributes to white matter change, lacunar infarcts and cognitive decline. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In confirmed resistant hypertension, which fourth agent has the most evidence behind it?","choices":["A second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Spironolactone, if filtration rate and potassium allow","A beta blocker","Hydralazine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it comes after the diuretic has been optimized and the renin-angiotensin blocker and dihydropyridine titrated to maximally tolerated doses rather than instead of doing so. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"How does initial drug selection differ between stage 1 and stage 2 hypertension?","choices":["One agent in stage 1; two complementary agents in stage 2","Two agents in stage 1 and a single agent in stage 2","Lifestyle alone in both stages","Four agents from the outset in every stage 2 patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and a single-pill combination is preferred where two are started, because adherence is itself one of the main determinants of whether control is achieved. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What is the standard home blood pressure monitoring schedule?","choices":["Continuous monitoring for twenty-four hours at home","Readings taken only when the patient feels unwell","Two readings morning and evening for seven days","One reading each morning for three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction not to overreact to the highest single reading is part of it: patients who act on outliers generate anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy. The two are taken at least a minute apart and the average is reported. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is resistant hypertension defined?","choices":["Severe blood pressure elevation with acute organ injury","Above goal despite three complementary drugs at maximal doses","Blood pressure above goal despite any two medications","Blood pressure that rises again after an initially good response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and each part of that carries weight: three agents, from complementary classes, and at doses the patient actually tolerates. A patient on three drugs at low doses has not yet met the definition. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pattern of renal artery stenosis predominates in older patients with established vascular disease?","choices":["Atherosclerotic stenosis","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Vasculitis of the renal arteries","Congenital renal artery hypoplasia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it contrasts with fibromuscular dysplasia, which is an important cause in younger women and is often amenable to percutaneous revascularization. It is often bilateral and progressive. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"When is a chest radiograph appropriate in hypertension?","choices":["When pulmonary edema or cardiomegaly is suspected","Routinely at diagnosis in every hypertensive patient","Only to exclude aortic dissection","Annually for surveillance of cardiac size and shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is explicitly not indicated routinely for uncomplicated hypertension. The principle attached is to order imaging to answer a specific clinical question. A suspected widened mediastinum is the third indication. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How is obstructive sleep apnea confirmed once suspected?","choices":["Measurement of morning plasma aldosterone","Polysomnography or home sleep apnea testing","The STOP-BANG questionnaire alone","Overnight blood pressure monitoring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive screening questionnaire raises suspicion but does not establish the diagnosis, and treating the apnea does not remove the need to continue indicated antihypertensive therapy. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How should the patient be positioned during measurement?","choices":["Lying flat with the arm above the head","Back supported, feet flat, arm at heart level","Legs crossed with the arm resting in the lap","Standing, with the arm hanging at the side"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction not to speak is easily forgotten, yet conversation during the measurement is one of the routine ways an office reading is inflated. The legs stay uncrossed and the patient does not speak. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What defines a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["A systolic pressure above a defined absolute threshold","Any blood pressure that remains above goal despite three drugs","Blood pressure elevation with any symptom, including headache","Severe elevation with acute hypertension-mediated organ injury"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The injury defines it rather than the number, and no absolute numeric cutoff exists. A very high pressure with no acute organ involvement is not an emergency, which is the distinction that decides how fast the pressure is brought down. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which substances should a medication review specifically ask about beyond prescribed drugs?","choices":["Over-the-counter drugs, licorice and supplements","Only prescribed antihypertensive agents","Only agents started within the past week","Only intravenous medications given in hospital"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients frequently do not regard any of these as medicines, so a review confined to the prescription list will miss the cause in exactly the patient whose pressure is otherwise unexplained. Recreational substances belong in the same review. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does obstructive sleep apnea raise blood pressure?","choices":["Autonomous aldosterone secretion","Direct mechanical compression of the renal arteries","Intermittent hypoxemia driving sympathetic activation","Impaired sodium excretion expanding extracellular volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it explains the characteristic pattern: the pressure is often resistant or specifically nocturnal, because the driver is operating overnight rather than through the day. Recurrent airway obstruction and repeated arousal are what produce it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which modifiable factors raise the risk of developing hypertension?","choices":["Advancing age, family history and ancestry","Male sex, independent of anything else","A history of childhood streptococcal infection","Excess weight, high sodium, inactivity and alcohol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and each maps onto a lifestyle intervention, which is why lifestyle treatment is recommended at every blood pressure category rather than only once medication has begun. Low potassium intake and poor sleep belong on the list too. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the commonest adverse effect of a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker?","choices":["Hyperkalaemia","Bradycardia and atrioventricular block","Dependent ankle edema","Dry cough"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside flushing, headache and gingival enlargement. The edema follows from arterial vasodilation rather than from fluid overload, which is why a diuretic does not fix it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which of these is an example of the acute injury that makes severe hypertension an emergency?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy on the electrocardiogram","Albuminuria on a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio","Arteriovenous nicking on fundoscopy","Hypertensive encephalopathy or acute pulmonary edema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Brain, lung, kidney, aorta and heart, which is why the assessment of a severely hypertensive patient is a survey of several organs rather than a single test. Any one of them converts a number into an emergency. The full list also includes acute kidney injury, aortic dissection and acute coronary syndrome. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why might a patient with pheochromocytoma have orthostatic symptoms despite being hypertensive?","choices":["Beta blockade has been started first","Concurrent adrenal insufficiency is always present","Catecholamine-induced vasoconstriction produces volume contraction","The tumor secretes aldosterone as well as catecholamines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is a genuinely counterintuitive combination: the same catecholamine excess that raises the pressure also shrinks the circulating volume, so the patient is hypertensive and orthostatic at once. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which adverse effects and cautions attach to ACE inhibitors?","choices":["Dry cough, hyperkalemia, creatinine rise and angioedema","Dependent ankle edema, flushing and headache","Hyperuricemia precipitating gout","Sedation, dry mouth and rebound hypertension on withdrawal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cough is bradykinin-mediated, which is why switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker usually resolves it while keeping the same therapeutic effect. They are avoided in pregnancy, in prior angioedema and in bilateral renal artery stenosis. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How may hypertensive heart disease progress?","choices":["To heart failure with preserved or reduced ejection fraction","Only to heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","Only to valvular heart disease","To pericardial effusion and tamponade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both routes are open, which is why a normal ejection fraction on echocardiography does not reassure: preserved-fraction failure is a recognized endpoint of the same process. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How should a patient be prepared before blood pressure is measured?","choices":["Measure immediately on arrival to capture a realistic reading","Take a single reading to avoid alarming the patient","Use any available cuff regardless of arm size","Rest quietly for five minutes and empty the bladder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Technique is not a formality here: getting it wrong is one of the first things to exclude before labeling a patient as resistant, because measurement error mimics true resistance. Smoking, caffeine and vigorous activity are avoided for thirty minutes beforehand. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"By what mechanisms does hypertension accelerate atherosclerosis?","choices":["Reduced cardiac output limiting vessel perfusion","Endothelial shear stress and intimal injury","Direct calcification of the arterial media","Immune complex deposition in the vessel wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is why the management principle attached is to treat total cardiovascular risk rather than blood pressure alone, with antiplatelet and lipid-lowering therapy where indicated. Oxidation of low-density lipoprotein and smooth muscle proliferation follow. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which drug classes are first-line for uncomplicated hypertension?","choices":["Spironolactone, clonidine and loop diuretics","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and beta blockers","Thiazide diuretics, renin-angiotensin blockers and dihydropyridines","Beta blockers, thiazide-type diuretics and hydralazine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and beta blockers are deliberately absent from that list: they are reserved for patients whose comorbidities, such as reduced ejection fraction or ischemic disease, select them. That means ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers among the three. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"}]};
